copies the new subdirectory. Only the CHANGELOG (\\\"installs and upgrades receive the packages\\\") and the shared exclusion predicate support that.\\n- Infrastructure faults fail open:\\n  - A missing or unreadable fragment raises at import, leaving no stdout and exit 1. Under Claude Code the shim exec's the hook (_python-hook.sh:416), so that is a non-blocking error.\\n  - An empty or truncated fragment ends in a NameError, either at import or inside main()'s try, which emits allow.\\n  - The v1.4.2 monolith behaved the same way on its hard _lib imports, so this is not a new class of fault.\\n  - The model rail cannot empty a fragment, because the kernel hard-deny and the canonical guard both cover it.\\n- The input-side fail-closed paths moved byte-for-byte: the _e3 shlex fail-closed, the _e4 unparseable-toggle check, the citation verifier, and the credential-scan fail-closed.\\n- Moved code keeps its behavior. exec(code, globals(), globals()) gives every function the facade's __globals__, so these still work:\\n  - monkeypatch points, e.g. policy-shadow-runner.py:189-207 swaps _check_credential_leak;\\n  - the dataclasses' __module__;\\n  - __file__-relative sys.path (normalization.py:190 still resolves to .claude/hooks);\\n  - the delayed check_canonical_edit import.\\n  compile() also inherits the facade's `annotations` future flag. decisions_cli.py:252 keeps the `if __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\"` guard, and it runs last, after all eight fragments are loaded. No __all__ existed before or after.\\n- Writes: the facade itself writes nothing.\\n- Python: standard library only, and compatible with 3.9 (pathlib, typing.Tuple, the exec/compile builtins).\\n\\nP0: none.\\n\\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): no undeclared P1 found in this payload.\\n\\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\\n1. P2 (comment points to the wrong file), check_bash_safety.py:12. The facade docstring says \\\"def _apply_destructive_citation_gate — implementation in rewrite_citation.py\\\". The function is actually defined at _lib/_bash_safety_parts/fact_state.py:4. Condition 7 and the CHANGELOG tell adopters to re-apply hand patches \\\"in the module\\\", so this pointer sends a maintainer to the wrong file. Fix: change the line to fact_state.py.\\n2. P2 (fragment layout is hard to navigate), _bash_safety_parts. The file names do not match their contents; the split looks driven by line count:\\n   - posture_tables.py:4 opens with _e3_check_canonical_path_write, the canonical-path write interceptor;\\n   - fact_canonical.py holds _apply_fact_gate plus the E3 tables;\\n   - fact_state.py holds the ADR-175 citation gate.\\n   Fix: add a \\\"definition → fragment\\\" map to the facade docstring or ADR-202, or rename the fragments to match what they hold.\\n3. P2 (__doc__ is overwritten; a doc pointer is stale), check_bash_safety.py:34-39 with each fragment's line-1 docstring. exec of a module code object stores the leading string in namespace[\\\"__doc__\\\"]. After _install_parts(), check_bash_safety.__doc__ is therefore decisions_cli's \\\"Decision conversion, audit emitters, and hook entry point.\\\", not the facade's docstring. settings.json:147 still says \\\"See check_bash_safety.py docstring for full logic\\\", but that text now sits as an inert string in normalization.py:5-55. Fix: save and restore __doc__ around the exec loop, and repoint the settings comment.\\n4. P2 (docstring overstates purity; a direct-caller edge), posture_scan.py:474 and :571. decide_command is still documented as \\\"Pure decision function — no I/O\\\", but it now does a lazy `from _lib import bash_indirect_guard` outside any try. In a partial upgrade without bash_indirect_guard.py, every command that passes the legacy rules raises ImportError:\\n   - In the hook, main() logs FATAL and allows. That direction is correct for an infrastructure fault, and the legacy blocks still run first.\\n   - Direct callers get an exception instead of a Decision: policy-shadow-runner.py:199 on an otherwise-allowed command (self_test.py:314 is unaffected, because \\\"rm -rf /\\\" is blocked earlier).\\n   Fix: do the import inside the existing try and treat ImportError as \\\"guard unavailable\\\" with a stderr breadcrumb; correct the docstring.\\n5. P2 (load-order invariant is untested), check_bash_safety.py:20-23. The entry point runs main() from inside the exec loop, through decisions_cli's __main__ guard. Any fragment appended after \\\"decisions_cli\\\" would load only after main() has already run and exited. Fix: drop the guard from decisions_cli, put `if __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\": sys.exit(main())` in the facade after _install_parts(), or assert that _PARTS[-1] == \\\"decisions_cli\\\".\\n6. P2 (stale maintainer tooling), .claude/governance/function-length-grandfather.yaml:440-451. The grandfather entries still tie _normalize_command_tokens and _check_rm_rf to .claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py (lines 86 and 208). Both functions now live in _bash_safety_parts/normalization.py. In enforcing mode, check-function-length.py would report them as new violations, and the old entries are dead. Fix: re-key the entries to the fragment paths.\",\"decision\":\"GO\",\"sentence\":\"The declared conditions this payload touches (5, 6 and 7) hold against the code, the facade's import and exec path fails open on infrastructure faults and keeps every input-side fail-closed matcher, and the only findings are P2 documentation and maintainability follow-ups, with no P0 and no undeclared P1.\"}","structured_output":{"report":"PAYLOAD 21/32: .claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py goes from a 4,237-line monolith to a 42-line facade. The facade execs eight source fragments from .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/ into its own globals.\n\nSCOPE AND METHOD\nI read the facade and all nine files in _bash_safety_parts (the eight fragments plus __init__.py). I checked every fragment boundary for a statement or decorator split across files; none is. I compared the moved declarations with the base text in the 